1
00:00:02,211 --> 00:00:04,213
[ominous, pensive music playing]

2
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:23,649
[ominous, pensive music continues]

3
00:00:33,242 --> 00:00:35,368
[raider 1 speaks indistinctly]

4
00:00:35,369 --> 00:00:36,745
[raider 2 speaks indistinctly]

5
00:00:42,084 --> 00:00:44,086
[suspenseful music playing]

6
00:00:46,171 --> 00:00:48,173
- You want to tell us why we--
- You're going out.

7
00:00:50,926 --> 00:00:52,761
I need to send you guys out at dawn.

8
00:00:54,221 --> 00:00:55,471
To do what?

9
00:00:55,472 --> 00:00:57,849
- We're going to the other silo.
- [Juliette] Yeah.

10
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:01,269
I told Camille that I was gonna send
you there to bring the kids back, but I--

11
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:03,563
But you really want us to find out
where the poison is coming in.

12
00:01:03,564 --> 00:01:05,857
- [Juliette] Right.
- Are you fucking kidding me?

13
00:01:05,858 --> 00:01:07,859
Look, I can go by myself.

14
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:10,444
- No, I need two people on the job.
- [Kennedy] Wait, let me guess.

15
00:01:10,445 --> 00:01:13,030
- You need two in case one of us dies.
- Yeah, in case one of you dies.

16
00:01:13,031 --> 00:01:15,992
- [Kennedy] Great.
- Kennedy, I don't get you.

17
00:01:15,993 --> 00:01:18,995
You spent three months screaming,
"Let us out."

18
00:01:18,996 --> 00:01:21,330
And now you don't wanna go out?

19
00:01:21,331 --> 00:01:25,669
No, no, no. I wanna go out. I just don't
give a shit about the rest of it.

20
00:01:26,962 --> 00:01:28,213
[scoffs]

21
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:35,261
Here's an idea. I get Lukie here
safely to 17, can I do what I want?

22
00:01:35,262 --> 00:01:36,512
And what is it that you want?

23
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:41,559
I want to go anywhere
but another fucking hole in the ground.

24
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,895
[Juliette] Okay, yeah.

25
00:01:43,896 --> 00:01:48,108
You get Lukas to 17, you could do
whatever you want. I don't care.

26
00:01:48,859 --> 00:01:51,277
That's great. Let's suit up.

27
00:01:51,278 --> 00:01:52,446
[knocks on door]

28
00:02:01,705 --> 00:02:03,707
[suspenseful music playing]

29
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:05,375
[door closes]

30
00:02:19,431 --> 00:02:21,433
All right, uh, keep them out of sight.

31
00:02:22,309 --> 00:02:23,851
I need you to get Danny Bly.

32
00:02:23,852 --> 00:02:27,104
I need him to shut down the screens
in the cafeteria. Okay?

33
00:02:27,105 --> 00:02:28,606
- Okay.
- Okay.

34
00:02:28,607 --> 00:02:29,691
I'll be back.

35
00:02:44,331 --> 00:02:45,666
- Hey.
- Hey.

36
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:47,251
What's up?

37
00:02:49,795 --> 00:02:51,587
I went to Water Filtration.

38
00:02:51,588 --> 00:02:53,673
There were guards outside,

39
00:02:53,674 --> 00:02:57,552
which means
the drugs are already in the water.

40
00:02:57,553 --> 00:02:59,136
How long until they take effect?

41
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:01,682
According to Bernard, immediately.

42
00:03:02,599 --> 00:03:08,689
Start drinking milk, juice, wine,
anything except what comes out of the tap.

43
00:03:09,481 --> 00:03:12,693
Whatever you're going to do, do it now.

44
00:03:17,155 --> 00:03:18,615
I'm being followed.

45
00:03:19,783 --> 00:03:22,411
Both on foot and by camera.

46
00:03:23,829 --> 00:03:24,830
So,

47
00:03:25,998 --> 00:03:28,166
we can't have any further contact.

48
00:03:34,089 --> 00:03:35,757
Hey, Robert.

49
00:03:38,594 --> 00:03:39,678
Be careful.

50
00:03:47,644 --> 00:03:49,980
- [whistling]
- [door opens]

51
00:03:51,481 --> 00:03:52,900
What are you two doing here?

52
00:03:54,193 --> 00:03:56,527
We were with Danny
when the deputy messaged.

53
00:03:56,528 --> 00:03:57,821
What's going on?

54
00:03:58,405 --> 00:03:59,488
I'm going out.

55
00:03:59,489 --> 00:04:01,324
Uh, we're going out.

56
00:04:01,325 --> 00:04:03,784
- Oh, yeah. W-We're going out.
- What, why?

57
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:09,332
Well, uh, when Juliette went out,
she actually went to another silo.

58
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:10,917
[Kennedy] They know that.

59
00:04:10,918 --> 00:04:13,711
The numbers in the helmets.
She went to Silo number 17.

60
00:04:13,712 --> 00:04:17,965
Right, and 17 was basically dead,
except for some kids,

61
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:20,301
and she promised
that she'd bring them back.

62
00:04:20,302 --> 00:04:21,303
Yeah.

63
00:04:23,347 --> 00:04:24,681
You still wanna go out?

64
00:04:25,349 --> 00:04:26,808
[Kennedy chuckles] Yeah.

65
00:04:28,477 --> 00:04:30,646
Maybe you don't want to anymore, Kat, but...

66
00:04:32,189 --> 00:04:36,734
but Danny and me,
we saw that cleaning video.

67
00:04:36,735 --> 00:04:37,736
[scoffs]

68
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:40,697
Maybe that's something
in the glass of her helmet.

69
00:04:41,448 --> 00:04:42,573
No.

70
00:04:42,574 --> 00:04:44,075
You said that was possible.

71
00:04:44,076 --> 00:04:46,453
I don't... I don't know
if it's good or bad out there.

72
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:52,584
I know someone who'd be
excited you get to go out.

73
00:04:54,253 --> 00:04:56,922
[chuckles] Yeah.

74
00:04:57,422 --> 00:04:59,424
[pensive music playing]

75
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:03,428
[Danny] You guys ever meet Kennedy's wife?

76
00:05:06,181 --> 00:05:08,100
What do you think she'd say if it was her?

77
00:05:09,309 --> 00:05:13,145
Oh, she'd only talk like that
if she had a couple of drinks in her.

78
00:05:13,146 --> 00:05:15,648
[Danny] Doris wouldn't actually say
she wanted to go out.

79
00:05:15,649 --> 00:05:17,985
- She was smarter than that.
- She's smarter than anybody.

80
00:05:18,777 --> 00:05:22,154
She said if she ever killed someone...
[chuckles]

81
00:05:22,155 --> 00:05:23,573
[chuckles]

82
00:05:23,574 --> 00:05:26,033
- ...probably him.
- [chuckling] Probably.

83
00:05:26,034 --> 00:05:28,119
[chuckles] And if she ever got caught...

84
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:29,912
She'd have got away with it.

85
00:05:29,913 --> 00:05:34,084
She said that if she got sent out
to clean, she'd go out singing,

86
00:05:34,668 --> 00:05:37,754
happy to be free, be outside.

87
00:05:38,422 --> 00:05:41,842
[inhales sharply] Even if it was just
for one fucking minute.

88
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:47,014
One fucking minute.

89
00:05:49,516 --> 00:05:51,018
[sighs]

90
00:05:51,518 --> 00:05:53,103
[door opens]

91
00:05:53,729 --> 00:05:55,272
[Jerry] The suit-techs are ready.

92
00:05:58,817 --> 00:05:59,860
Let's do it.

93
00:06:02,362 --> 00:06:04,364
[suspenseful music playing]

94
00:06:25,969 --> 00:06:27,971
[suspenseful music building]

95
00:06:30,933 --> 00:06:32,600
I know I said you had until dawn,

96
00:06:32,601 --> 00:06:34,603
I need you to go now. Come on, hurry up.

97
00:06:48,283 --> 00:06:49,284
It's off.

98
00:06:50,744 --> 00:06:53,579
All right. We'll keep the radio on
all of the time.

99
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:57,124
You'll hear me through your helmet here,
and I'll hear you on the radio,

100
00:06:57,125 --> 00:06:58,918
but remember to talk in code.

101
00:06:58,919 --> 00:07:00,211
Are you gonna be able to hear me?

102
00:07:00,212 --> 00:07:01,295
I hope not.

103
00:07:01,296 --> 00:07:02,713
- What?
- So, who am I?

104
00:07:02,714 --> 00:07:05,341
You are dispatch. I'm Deputy Turley.

105
00:07:05,342 --> 00:07:06,509
- She's Crane.
- Yep.

106
00:07:06,510 --> 00:07:08,344
Uh, Im-I'm never gonna remember that.

107
00:07:08,345 --> 00:07:10,388
- Can I be something other than Calso?
- Hey, Patrick. Hey.

108
00:07:10,389 --> 00:07:12,932
Calm. We don't have time for that, okay?

109
00:07:12,933 --> 00:07:16,519
Walk is only gonna talk to Lukas.
You're gonna get him to 17.

110
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:18,814
That's it. Okay?

111
00:07:27,281 --> 00:07:29,658
He'll be fine. [inhales sharply]

112
00:07:30,242 --> 00:07:33,287
[air lock door closes]

113
00:07:40,043 --> 00:07:42,171
[air hissing]

114
00:07:44,423 --> 00:07:46,758
[suspenseful music playing]

115
00:08:01,690 --> 00:08:04,026
[Lukas breathing heavily]

116
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:21,668
This is dispatch to Deputy Turley.
How do you read?

117
00:08:22,169 --> 00:08:26,131
[Lukas] Uh, um... w-well...

118
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:29,968
Uh, the situation, uh...

119
00:08:33,304 --> 00:08:36,140
Uh, it's a lot bigger than expected.

120
00:08:42,981 --> 00:08:45,067
[chuckles]

121
00:08:47,569 --> 00:08:48,820
There's nothing.

122
00:08:51,031 --> 00:08:52,449
It's just nothing.

123
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:54,701
There's just nothing!

124
00:08:57,162 --> 00:08:58,163
Hey!

125
00:09:06,547 --> 00:09:08,172
What the fuck is all that?

126
00:09:08,173 --> 00:09:09,174
[chuckles]

127
00:09:39,788 --> 00:09:41,248
[Lukas, on radio] <i>Deputy Turley here.</i>

128
00:09:43,584 --> 00:09:45,669
We're coming up on that next level.

129
00:09:47,838 --> 00:09:50,424
[footsteps on radio]

130
00:09:57,723 --> 00:09:59,558
[breathes heavily]

131
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:03,812
[breathes heavily]

132
00:10:09,902 --> 00:10:10,903
[Lukas] Dispatch.

133
00:10:12,738 --> 00:10:15,908
We see the... the damage.

134
00:10:17,409 --> 00:10:19,286
<i>It's way worse than we imagined.</i>

135
00:10:21,121 --> 00:10:23,332
<i>But we're-- we're gonna investigate.</i>

136
00:10:24,166 --> 00:10:25,250
Copy that.

137
00:10:30,547 --> 00:10:32,299
[key buzzing]

138
00:10:43,352 --> 00:10:44,686
[breathes heavily]

139
00:10:52,986 --> 00:10:54,238
[breathing heavily]

140
00:10:59,409 --> 00:11:02,162
[loud buzzing]

141
00:11:03,038 --> 00:11:05,331
- [Kennedy] Do you hear that?
- What?

142
00:11:05,332 --> 00:11:06,542
Do you hear that?

143
00:11:10,671 --> 00:11:14,591
Hey, dispatch. [stammers]
We're hearing a-a-a buzzing sound.

144
00:11:15,133 --> 00:11:16,552
<i>Can you hear it?</i>

145
00:11:17,261 --> 00:11:19,680
[buzzing on radio]

146
00:11:21,557 --> 00:11:23,559
[buzzing intensifies]

147
00:11:26,728 --> 00:11:29,814
- [Lukas] <i>Oh, fuck. Run! Run!</i>
- [gunfire]

148
00:11:29,815 --> 00:11:30,983
[Lukas grunts, screams]

149
00:11:32,985 --> 00:11:34,485
[radio static]

150
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:38,615
Deputy Turley, this is dispatch.
If you read this, acknowledge.

151
00:11:42,244 --> 00:11:45,414
Deputy Calso,
if you read this, acknowledge.

152
00:11:49,209 --> 00:11:50,626
What happened? [sniffles]

153
00:11:50,627 --> 00:11:52,879
It sounded like... guns.

154
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:54,505
- They have guns out there?
- [Juliette] No.

155
00:11:54,506 --> 00:11:55,923
Radio them again.

156
00:11:55,924 --> 00:11:58,177
There's no static on the line. It's just...

157
00:12:01,138 --> 00:12:02,890
[breathes shakily]

158
00:12:06,018 --> 00:12:08,228
[inhales sharply] Okay, um...

159
00:12:09,438 --> 00:12:13,025
I don't know. Danny, can you just
get the screens back on, please?

160
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:16,736
I don't know. What if you
go down to the workshop,

161
00:12:16,737 --> 00:12:18,614
and I'll come and meet you
if I-- if I can?

162
00:12:19,531 --> 00:12:20,740
- Where will you go?
- I don't know,

163
00:12:20,741 --> 00:12:23,701
but if I run, she's gonna arrest me.
So I-I have to stay.

164
00:12:23,702 --> 00:12:26,495
S-So if, um... When she gets here,

165
00:12:26,496 --> 00:12:29,166
Sandy, can you just say that I-I... I'm...
I've gone back home?

166
00:12:29,666 --> 00:12:31,251
Jerry, is the combination the same?

167
00:12:31,835 --> 00:12:33,337
[breathes shakily]

168
00:12:41,053 --> 00:12:42,136
[beeps]

169
00:12:42,137 --> 00:12:45,015
[The Algorithm] <i>Lukas Kyle
and Patrick Kennedy have been neutralized.</i>

170
00:12:48,268 --> 00:12:49,353
What happened?

171
00:12:51,563 --> 00:12:54,358
[The Algorithm]
<i>You saw Nichols's unedited cleaning video.</i>

172
00:12:55,025 --> 00:12:56,276
<i>What did you see?</i>

173
00:12:56,777 --> 00:12:57,945
Bodies.

174
00:12:59,446 --> 00:13:01,615
What looked like an entire silo's worth.

175
00:13:02,282 --> 00:13:04,034
<i>And what does that suggest?</i>

176
00:13:14,169 --> 00:13:16,505
They were able to prevent a Safeguard.

177
00:13:18,423 --> 00:13:20,258
At least momentarily.

178
00:13:20,259 --> 00:13:22,511
<i>"Momentarily" is the key word.</i>

179
00:13:24,221 --> 00:13:27,558
<i>It is important to know,
for your silo's sake,</i>

180
00:13:28,308 --> 00:13:32,145
<i>that the Safeguard is in fact
not preventable.</i>

181
00:13:32,729 --> 00:13:36,233
<i>Let what you saw in the video
be evidence of that fact.</i>

182
00:13:39,236 --> 00:13:40,487
But that's...

183
00:13:42,573 --> 00:13:44,741
that's what you think Nichols wants to do?

184
00:13:45,492 --> 00:13:46,618
Block the Safeguard?

185
00:13:47,703 --> 00:13:48,704
<i>Talk to her.</i>

186
00:13:49,538 --> 00:13:52,332
<i>Try to ascertain
if that fear is warranted.</i>

187
00:13:52,916 --> 00:13:54,001
If it is?

188
00:13:55,669 --> 00:13:56,920
[knocking on door]

189
00:14:01,967 --> 00:14:04,260
We agreed they'd be going out at dawn.

190
00:14:04,261 --> 00:14:08,307
Yeah, um, Kennedy was agitated,
so I needed to get him out.

191
00:14:08,807 --> 00:14:09,808
[Camille] Right.

192
00:14:10,726 --> 00:14:14,730
Well, I'm sorry to have to tell you this,
but they didn't make it to Silo 17.

193
00:14:18,483 --> 00:14:20,443
[inhales sharply]
Was there any other reason

194
00:14:20,444 --> 00:14:23,030
you wanted people from our silo
to go to 17?

195
00:14:23,530 --> 00:14:24,531
No.

196
00:14:25,532 --> 00:14:29,410
Those kids saved my life, and I wanted
to bring them back here, so that's why.

197
00:14:29,411 --> 00:14:31,330
- Nothing more important than that?
- Nope.

198
00:14:32,122 --> 00:14:36,710
Mm-mmm. What could be more important
than saving children's lives, Camille?

199
00:14:39,129 --> 00:14:41,173
Do you know what happened in Silo 17?

200
00:14:41,965 --> 00:14:43,675
[ominous music playing]

201
00:14:44,384 --> 00:14:46,220
[Juliette] No, do you?

202
00:14:47,679 --> 00:14:49,765
[Camille] No idea. That's why I asked.

203
00:14:53,435 --> 00:14:55,437
What the fuck happened out there, hmm?

204
00:14:55,938 --> 00:14:57,355
To Lukas and Kennedy.

205
00:14:57,356 --> 00:14:59,106
Suit malfunction, I believe.

206
00:14:59,107 --> 00:15:00,692
- Suit malfunction?
- Yeah.

207
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:02,903
That's what happens when you rush.

208
00:15:05,781 --> 00:15:06,782
Yeah.

209
00:15:07,824 --> 00:15:11,453
We should've given them more time
to make the suits bulletproof.

210
00:15:17,793 --> 00:15:19,795
[ominous music continues]

211
00:17:24,086 --> 00:17:25,170
Charlotte?

212
00:17:26,171 --> 00:17:27,755
What's happening? What are you doing here?

213
00:17:27,756 --> 00:17:29,257
Hey, um... [scoffs]

214
00:17:29,258 --> 00:17:32,301
- She can't tell you.
- I-I didn't actually ask you, yeah?

215
00:17:32,302 --> 00:17:33,303
Sure.

216
00:17:37,057 --> 00:17:38,141
Are you okay?

217
00:17:40,477 --> 00:17:41,895
I'm all right, yeah.

218
00:17:43,605 --> 00:17:45,231
Do you know who I am?

219
00:17:45,232 --> 00:17:46,984
Of course I do, Daniel.

220
00:17:47,901 --> 00:17:51,321
But I really can't say any more
until you do what Anna says.

221
00:17:51,947 --> 00:17:53,030
[Anna] If you would both

222
00:17:53,031 --> 00:17:55,825
- just follow me through these doors.
- Okay. After the ride we just had,

223
00:17:55,826 --> 00:17:58,369
- we're not going anywhere with anyone.
- What is through those doors?

224
00:17:58,370 --> 00:18:02,832
Lawyers. Many lawyers
with nondisclosure agreements.

225
00:18:02,833 --> 00:18:05,042
Once you've signed, we'll get on the plane

226
00:18:05,043 --> 00:18:07,837
and by this time tomorrow,
you'll know everything.

227
00:18:07,838 --> 00:18:11,424
However, you won't be able
to tell anybody any of it...

228
00:18:11,425 --> 00:18:13,676
- [phone keypad clacking]
- ...until we say so.

229
00:18:13,677 --> 00:18:15,470
- [phone chimes]
- What, you...

230
00:18:18,098 --> 00:18:21,684
You gonna sue me? [chuckles]
'Cause I have $700 in my bank account.

231
00:18:21,685 --> 00:18:25,730
Gitmo. Half of the lawyers in there
are government lawyers,

232
00:18:25,731 --> 00:18:29,026
which means that they can send you
to actual fucking Guantánamo.

233
00:18:31,278 --> 00:18:34,406
And I'm not kidding. [sighs]
I've been there.

234
00:18:35,532 --> 00:18:38,076
It's actually quite lovely.
I'm thinking about buying it.

235
00:18:38,994 --> 00:18:43,498
The island. The whole island,
not just the... the base.

236
00:18:45,209 --> 00:18:47,127
[exhales deeply] So...

237
00:18:48,921 --> 00:18:50,505
I'm Per Stensen.

238
00:18:50,506 --> 00:18:51,714
We know who you are.

239
00:18:51,715 --> 00:18:55,384
Oh, I know. I'm just being polite.
My social team says I need to do that.

240
00:18:55,385 --> 00:18:56,762
You're the journalist, right?

241
00:18:57,387 --> 00:18:59,388
I've been told
that you have a lot of questions,

242
00:18:59,389 --> 00:19:01,225
but I would love to ask you one.

243
00:19:02,267 --> 00:19:03,644
Which would you prefer,

244
00:19:04,978 --> 00:19:09,941
not signing the NDA and driving
out of here in a shitty rental minivan

245
00:19:09,942 --> 00:19:11,192
not knowing anything,

246
00:19:11,193 --> 00:19:14,237
or scribbling your name a few times

247
00:19:14,238 --> 00:19:18,158
and getting to know
the world's most important secret?

248
00:19:18,659 --> 00:19:20,661
[tense music playing]

249
00:19:21,411 --> 00:19:26,040
I'll throw in $100,000
if that moves the needle at all.

250
00:19:26,041 --> 00:19:27,500
$100,000. Of course.

251
00:19:27,501 --> 00:19:29,418
The richest man on the planet,

252
00:19:29,419 --> 00:19:33,005
and you're gonna give us
the world's largest secret for $100,000?

253
00:19:33,006 --> 00:19:34,674
Okay, fine. A million, each.

254
00:19:34,675 --> 00:19:37,051
- Oh, my... You can't just buy everybody.
- [Per] But only if you sign.

255
00:19:37,052 --> 00:19:39,637
- You don't get anything if you don't.
- I don't know what is going on here.

256
00:19:39,638 --> 00:19:42,683
All I want to know
is what the fuck happened to my sister.

257
00:19:43,976 --> 00:19:45,227
Did you sign?

258
00:19:47,479 --> 00:19:51,066
I did 'cause I also want to know
what the fuck happened to me.

259
00:19:59,408 --> 00:20:00,409
Yeah.

260
00:20:02,160 --> 00:20:05,622
So that's a yes from the congressman.
What say you, journalist?

261
00:20:09,042 --> 00:20:12,712
How long before I can write
about whatever this is?

262
00:20:12,713 --> 00:20:16,549
No more than five years. Hopefully less.

263
00:20:16,550 --> 00:20:19,468
And I wasn't just being dramatic before.

264
00:20:19,469 --> 00:20:22,181
This really is about saving the world.

265
00:20:29,104 --> 00:20:30,105
Sure. [sighs]

266
00:20:30,731 --> 00:20:32,191
Let's go save the world.

267
00:20:34,526 --> 00:20:36,612
[people speaking indistinctly]

268
00:20:41,658 --> 00:20:42,659
Ed Harwood.

269
00:20:45,162 --> 00:20:46,621
[Ed] What's going on?

270
00:20:46,622 --> 00:20:49,208
We need to speak with you, privately.

271
00:20:53,128 --> 00:20:56,757
We think Orla Kent was killed
and then moved.

272
00:20:58,050 --> 00:21:01,844
What we don't know
is how her body ended up here.

273
00:21:01,845 --> 00:21:05,640
Well, it sounds like those cameras
you said were being shut down weren't.

274
00:21:05,641 --> 00:21:10,144
Otherwise, how would you be so sure
she didn't just walk up the stairs?

275
00:21:10,145 --> 00:21:12,730
Do you have any idea how--

276
00:21:12,731 --> 00:21:15,733
How a body gets moved
without cameras picking it up?

277
00:21:15,734 --> 00:21:17,736
I do not. [sniffs]

278
00:21:18,570 --> 00:21:20,197
Unless...

279
00:21:22,032 --> 00:21:24,325
Yeah, there was this guy in the Mids.

280
00:21:24,326 --> 00:21:26,702
He used to put on a little show
for the kiddies.

281
00:21:26,703 --> 00:21:30,832
He'd make something disappear,
and then poof, appear again.

282
00:21:31,416 --> 00:21:34,544
Called it magic. [chuckles]

283
00:21:34,545 --> 00:21:38,048
Maybe that's what happened. Magic.

284
00:21:38,549 --> 00:21:42,093
We're wasting time. You want to go home--

285
00:21:42,094 --> 00:21:46,390
Look, Sheriff. You just don't get it.

286
00:21:47,808 --> 00:21:51,686
Here in the mines,
we got maybe six guys, two women,

287
00:21:51,687 --> 00:21:55,691
who are just so fucking criminal,
this is the only place for them. Fine.

288
00:21:56,441 --> 00:21:59,194
Mostly, it's just people
who did something stupid,

289
00:21:59,945 --> 00:22:03,699
and they get sent here,
they get crushed by a rockfall.

290
00:22:04,366 --> 00:22:07,536
Or they get fucking poisoned
by rat exterminators.

291
00:22:08,370 --> 00:22:13,457
Now, the guy who did the magic,
making an egg disappear for some kids,

292
00:22:13,458 --> 00:22:18,505
he was told by Judicial
that magic was a theoretical relic.

293
00:22:19,256 --> 00:22:23,093
A theoretical fucking relic.

294
00:22:23,677 --> 00:22:26,847
So they sent him here,
not for doing a thing,

295
00:22:27,347 --> 00:22:29,474
but for having an idea.

296
00:22:31,101 --> 00:22:33,478
What happens back in those tunnels, you...

297
00:22:34,146 --> 00:22:37,107
It would be more humane
to hang people like him,

298
00:22:38,025 --> 00:22:40,610
but no, they get sent here.

299
00:22:40,611 --> 00:22:45,199
Each and every one of them
by your good old buddies in Judicial.

300
00:22:47,993 --> 00:22:48,994
So...

301
00:22:50,996 --> 00:22:53,080
obviously you don't respect me as sheriff.

302
00:22:53,081 --> 00:22:56,335
[chuckling] Fuck no. [chuckles]

303
00:22:56,919 --> 00:22:58,336
You respect Juliette?

304
00:22:58,337 --> 00:23:01,589
At least she came from Mechanical
and had some dirt under her nails,

305
00:23:01,590 --> 00:23:04,801
but as sheriff? Nah.

306
00:23:05,385 --> 00:23:09,096
- Holston?
- [Ed] Holston? I respected him.

307
00:23:09,097 --> 00:23:12,935
If he caught some idiot doing something
stupid, you know, he might let it slide.

308
00:23:13,519 --> 00:23:15,686
He took into account people's families,

309
00:23:15,687 --> 00:23:17,563
and what a stint in the mines
would do to them.

310
00:23:17,564 --> 00:23:18,731
[Paul] Good.

311
00:23:18,732 --> 00:23:25,364
I'm glad you respected him because
he thought you were a lousy crook.

312
00:23:27,074 --> 00:23:31,703
He opened seven different
investigations into you.

313
00:23:33,121 --> 00:23:34,623
Did he get me on any of them?

314
00:23:35,415 --> 00:23:37,458
[tense music playing]

315
00:23:37,459 --> 00:23:41,547
Hmm? Well, not such a lousy crook then.

316
00:23:42,714 --> 00:23:44,340
[Paul] I saw you argue with Orla Kent

317
00:23:44,341 --> 00:23:47,510
the day before her body was found
in your mines. Your daughter--

318
00:23:47,511 --> 00:23:50,513
My daughter was lined up
to be the next head of Supply

319
00:23:50,514 --> 00:23:53,307
until Orla Kent took her job.

320
00:23:53,308 --> 00:23:55,393
So yeah, I was upset.

321
00:23:55,394 --> 00:23:58,855
Glenda had a chance
to have a life away from all this.

322
00:23:58,856 --> 00:24:01,983
But because a lot of people
in this fucking place

323
00:24:01,984 --> 00:24:04,777
assume things based on
where someone's from,

324
00:24:04,778 --> 00:24:06,988
or who they associate with,

325
00:24:06,989 --> 00:24:12,119
that chance was taken away and given
to the lovely Miss Kent.

326
00:24:13,078 --> 00:24:15,163
I said my piece to her, and that was it.

327
00:24:16,039 --> 00:24:20,294
So, unless you got more evidence
than what's in that folder,

328
00:24:20,878 --> 00:24:25,716
I would like to kindly ask you
to, uh, fuck off.

329
00:24:27,551 --> 00:24:30,012
[breathes shakily]

330
00:24:33,015 --> 00:24:35,474
Okay, you're here.
Come and have a look at this.

331
00:24:35,475 --> 00:24:38,019
We have a lot of gunpowder left over
from the rebellion.

332
00:24:38,020 --> 00:24:40,438
- We get a team into Judicial after hours...
- Right.

333
00:24:40,439 --> 00:24:42,231
...wearing GWP uniforms,

334
00:24:42,232 --> 00:24:44,942
and we scatter the explosives
along the wall to blow it open

335
00:24:44,943 --> 00:24:48,821
along with all the pipes behind it,
including the one that carries the poison.

336
00:24:48,822 --> 00:24:52,366
Okay, and then you release all the poison,
and then what? You have minutes to live?

337
00:24:52,367 --> 00:24:53,618
- We can wear cleaning suits...
- No.

338
00:24:53,619 --> 00:24:55,369
- ...under our GWP coveralls.
- Come on.

339
00:24:55,370 --> 00:24:56,537
We'll look like maintenance men.

340
00:24:56,538 --> 00:24:58,080
[Martha] We only have six suits left.

341
00:24:58,081 --> 00:25:01,709
[Teddy] Look, it'll be tough,
but with a good team, I can get it done.

342
00:25:01,710 --> 00:25:02,878
What about helmets?

343
00:25:03,462 --> 00:25:04,712
I can cover them.

344
00:25:04,713 --> 00:25:08,925
So how do we get six people
with helmets and bombs into Judicial?

345
00:25:08,926 --> 00:25:11,052
'Cause I don't think the coveralls
is gonna be enough.

346
00:25:11,053 --> 00:25:13,471
- Well, can Sims get us passes?
- [Juliette] But what about the gas?

347
00:25:13,472 --> 00:25:15,932
That gas could be odorless.
It could be colorless, right?

348
00:25:15,933 --> 00:25:19,769
The point is, Jules,
the new suits will give them an hour.

349
00:25:19,770 --> 00:25:21,604
Yeah, a lot of people can die in an hour.

350
00:25:21,605 --> 00:25:24,148
Did you think of that? Like, how many?

351
00:25:24,149 --> 00:25:27,193
[Knox] It depends on how long it takes
to cap the pipe,

352
00:25:27,194 --> 00:25:30,029
and how many people there are
on the levels above and below.

353
00:25:30,030 --> 00:25:33,200
Those levels are residential.
People will be home sleeping.

354
00:25:34,660 --> 00:25:36,495
[scoffs] Give us a range?

355
00:25:37,871 --> 00:25:41,374
[sighs] Anywhere from a hundred to--

356
00:25:41,375 --> 00:25:42,960
Could be a thousand.

357
00:25:44,545 --> 00:25:46,797
Thousand people. No.

358
00:25:49,716 --> 00:25:51,635
Better than 10,000.

359
00:25:52,386 --> 00:25:57,224
Maybe we should just drink the water
and forget the whole fucking thing.

360
00:26:00,227 --> 00:26:02,354
- Should we vote on this?
- [Bernard] No.

361
00:26:03,605 --> 00:26:09,987
[straining] The people of the Silo voted
when they made Juliette mayor.

362
00:26:14,575 --> 00:26:16,409
I need to think. I can't fucking...

363
00:26:16,410 --> 00:26:19,955
Can you guys just figure out a plan
that doesn't kill thousands of people?

364
00:26:21,415 --> 00:26:23,709
[door opens, closes]

365
00:26:31,341 --> 00:26:32,426
You good?

366
00:26:35,596 --> 00:26:37,097
There's something I gotta do.

367
00:26:45,564 --> 00:26:50,736
We started with the most recent memories,
being in the Navy, the mission.

368
00:26:51,778 --> 00:26:53,989
Iran. I thought he was gonna
leave that out.

369
00:26:55,324 --> 00:26:57,825
He said there would be value
in remembering

370
00:26:57,826 --> 00:26:59,620
right up to when I got into my plane...

371
00:27:01,705 --> 00:27:04,957
but we keep the rest in a vault. So...

372
00:27:04,958 --> 00:27:07,628
I know I flew the mission,
I know it went bad.

373
00:27:08,962 --> 00:27:13,382
I know the facts, but it's kinda
like it happened to someone else.

374
00:27:13,383 --> 00:27:14,801
Are you feeling okay?

375
00:27:15,302 --> 00:27:16,969
I'm better than I was.

376
00:27:16,970 --> 00:27:19,180
You know, Victor's helped me remember.

377
00:27:19,181 --> 00:27:20,766
But he also made you forget.

378
00:27:24,019 --> 00:27:25,521
I'm gonna try and get some sleep.

379
00:27:27,189 --> 00:27:28,190
Sure.

380
00:27:30,275 --> 00:27:32,736
Yeah, that was me asking you to move.
I need your seat.

381
00:27:34,404 --> 00:27:36,447
[exhales deeply] Okay.

382
00:27:36,448 --> 00:27:38,534
[gentle music playing]

383
00:27:43,747 --> 00:27:44,831
How is she?

384
00:27:46,124 --> 00:27:48,210
Well, she gave me some shit, so...

385
00:27:49,294 --> 00:27:50,504
So better?

386
00:27:51,380 --> 00:27:52,464
Maybe.

387
00:27:53,048 --> 00:27:55,092
[Mark] I knew it was gonna be a bad day.

388
00:27:55,592 --> 00:27:57,009
[Knox] How did my old man die?

389
00:27:57,010 --> 00:27:58,761
And a good day to you too.

390
00:27:58,762 --> 00:27:59,846
How did he die?

391
00:27:59,847 --> 00:28:01,514
Didn't you go to the mines already?

392
00:28:01,515 --> 00:28:03,349
Harwood said he got caught
in a tunnel collapse.

393
00:28:03,350 --> 00:28:05,018
- Then that's what happened.
- No.

394
00:28:05,936 --> 00:28:08,438
There was a bump in the way he said it.
There's something else.

395
00:28:11,483 --> 00:28:12,651
Please.

396
00:28:14,987 --> 00:28:15,988
Was he murdered?

397
00:28:16,488 --> 00:28:17,573
No.

398
00:28:20,325 --> 00:28:21,410
He's not dead.

399
00:28:22,995 --> 00:28:26,081
Someone got buried in the mines,
but it wasn't your dad.

400
00:28:27,958 --> 00:28:29,042
Way I heard it,

401
00:28:30,294 --> 00:28:32,503
your father designed something
for Harwood.

402
00:28:32,504 --> 00:28:37,342
In exchange, the next person to die
in the mines didn't officially die.

403
00:28:38,135 --> 00:28:39,468
And your old man took his place.

404
00:28:39,469 --> 00:28:41,137
What's the guy's name who died?

405
00:28:41,138 --> 00:28:43,723
You start digging around
in Harwood's business,

406
00:28:43,724 --> 00:28:45,893
you're likely to disappear
down a deep hole.

407
00:28:48,645 --> 00:28:49,771
Do you know his name?

408
00:28:54,776 --> 00:28:55,986
You wanted to see me?

409
00:28:59,656 --> 00:29:03,785
Do you know where the Mission diary is?

410
00:29:06,788 --> 00:29:10,042
I'm clearly no longer a threat.

411
00:29:12,377 --> 00:29:13,879
Old habits and all that.

412
00:29:15,172 --> 00:29:19,176
[Bernard] If you know where it is
and who has it,

413
00:29:20,177 --> 00:29:21,929
bring them both here.

414
00:29:24,681 --> 00:29:25,682
Please.

415
00:29:34,483 --> 00:29:35,651
Can't sleep?

416
00:29:38,987 --> 00:29:40,113
Uh...

417
00:29:40,614 --> 00:29:45,201
Daniel and I slept in the back
of the minivan when you were driving it.

418
00:29:45,202 --> 00:29:48,455
It wasn't me driving it,
and I know you didn't sleep.

419
00:29:50,749 --> 00:29:52,084
It was bugged?

420
00:29:55,796 --> 00:29:56,839
All right.

421
00:29:57,464 --> 00:30:00,801
I didn't know what really went on
with your <i>Post</i> piece.

422
00:30:01,552 --> 00:30:02,760
Good for you.

423
00:30:02,761 --> 00:30:05,013
Yeah, well, don't tell anyone.

424
00:30:06,223 --> 00:30:09,476
The NDAs cover everything
about everyone anywhere near this.

425
00:30:11,854 --> 00:30:12,980
What's your story?

426
00:30:15,524 --> 00:30:16,816
You wanna write a profile?

427
00:30:16,817 --> 00:30:20,112
This is just my default mode.
Everybody's interesting to me.

428
00:30:21,238 --> 00:30:25,117
I did my 20 in military intelligence.

429
00:30:25,868 --> 00:30:29,328
Left. Started a firm,
which was very successful.

430
00:30:29,329 --> 00:30:30,539
[inhales deeply]

431
00:30:31,874 --> 00:30:33,292
Until it wasn't.

432
00:30:34,459 --> 00:30:36,044
Entirely my fault.

433
00:30:36,753 --> 00:30:39,673
Certain character defects
I hadn't addressed.

434
00:30:40,674 --> 00:30:42,842
That's when I met the, uh... the senator.

435
00:30:42,843 --> 00:30:45,053
It was her first congressional campaign.

436
00:30:46,930 --> 00:30:48,140
I was her driver.

437
00:30:50,684 --> 00:30:53,854
And now you're her loyal... what?

438
00:30:54,646 --> 00:30:56,397
I don't really have a job title.

439
00:30:56,398 --> 00:30:59,525
I just make sure that the senator
and her daughter are all right.

440
00:30:59,526 --> 00:31:00,736
What does that mean?

441
00:31:03,405 --> 00:31:04,698
It means a lot.

442
00:31:17,211 --> 00:31:19,213
[pensive music playing]

443
00:31:20,088 --> 00:31:21,381
What are you doing out here?

444
00:31:22,549 --> 00:31:25,968
- You okay?
- I'm out here because we have a visitor.

445
00:31:25,969 --> 00:31:30,181
She's had an experience
not dissimilar to your own.

446
00:31:30,182 --> 00:31:32,892
What do you mean?
Camille drugged her as well?

447
00:31:32,893 --> 00:31:33,977
[Bernard] Not Camille.

448
00:31:35,020 --> 00:31:36,188
Robert and I...

449
00:31:38,065 --> 00:31:39,815
we took her off the drugs

450
00:31:39,816 --> 00:31:42,528
so that she could help the nurse
swap out your pills,

451
00:31:43,529 --> 00:31:46,114
because she's the only one...

452
00:31:48,158 --> 00:31:51,203
that can genuinely understand
what you've been through.

453
00:31:51,870 --> 00:31:58,210
And I think she has a unique perspective
of the future facing us all.

454
00:32:03,507 --> 00:32:04,883
Juliette Nichols.

455
00:32:06,468 --> 00:32:07,803
Gloria Hildebrandt.

456
00:32:12,140 --> 00:32:13,725
It's okay if you don't remember me.

457
00:32:14,226 --> 00:32:15,726
They had me on those drugs so long,

458
00:32:15,727 --> 00:32:18,938
I wasn't sure I'd-I'd ever remember
a single soul.

459
00:32:18,939 --> 00:32:23,360
I asked Mrs. Hildebrandt here
because we need to talk about memory.

460
00:32:24,486 --> 00:32:26,905
And because of what she holds
in her hands.

461
00:32:27,865 --> 00:32:29,199
When I was head of IT,

462
00:32:30,576 --> 00:32:36,331
Robert and I would track down relics
rumored to be sacred

463
00:32:37,124 --> 00:32:42,963
for a secret group of citizens
known as the Flamekeepers.

464
00:32:44,381 --> 00:32:46,341
George's mother was one of us.

465
00:32:48,343 --> 00:32:52,764
[Bernard] People deemed a threat,
who sought knowledge of the before times.

466
00:32:53,640 --> 00:32:58,644
And a revival of hope for a future
in a livable world outside.

467
00:32:58,645 --> 00:33:01,565
We wanted just the truth, like you.

468
00:33:02,232 --> 00:33:04,526
[Bernard] The two items we sought the most

469
00:33:05,194 --> 00:33:08,947
were both documents of life
from a particular time.

470
00:33:09,448 --> 00:33:12,617
A spiral-bound notepad

471
00:33:12,618 --> 00:33:17,539
the previous heads of IT pursued
for 300 years.

472
00:33:18,165 --> 00:33:19,332
It was never found.

473
00:33:19,333 --> 00:33:22,127
The other was an obsession of mine.

474
00:33:23,837 --> 00:33:25,047
The Mission diary.

475
00:33:26,423 --> 00:33:27,507
What mission?

476
00:33:27,508 --> 00:33:31,677
Mission was his name.
He was a porter, lived 140 years ago.

477
00:33:31,678 --> 00:33:33,513
And during the great rebellion,

478
00:33:33,514 --> 00:33:36,642
he transported the explosives
that were used to blow up the stairs.

479
00:33:37,267 --> 00:33:39,186
Your friends weren't the first to do that.

480
00:33:39,895 --> 00:33:43,398
[Bernard] Mrs. Hildebrandt,
would you read from near the end?

481
00:33:49,988 --> 00:33:52,533
[paper rustles]

482
00:33:55,869 --> 00:34:01,083
"I have a mark on my neck
and a scar on my stomach,

483
00:34:01,625 --> 00:34:04,502
and I don't know where they came from.

484
00:34:04,503 --> 00:34:08,339
I feel like I did know,
maybe even yesterday,

485
00:34:08,340 --> 00:34:10,842
but now I can't quite remember yesterday.

486
00:34:11,969 --> 00:34:13,219
I am afraid.

487
00:34:14,388 --> 00:34:16,472
We've all forgotten so much.

488
00:34:16,473 --> 00:34:20,561
Doctors keep saying it's a virus,
and it'll pass,

489
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:22,855
but it hasn't.

490
00:34:23,688 --> 00:34:25,774
Things keep slipping away.

491
00:34:26,358 --> 00:34:30,195
And anything I still remember
has gone hazy, like in a dream.

492
00:34:30,904 --> 00:34:34,657
I don't know what I'd do without Allie.
We have each other.

493
00:34:34,658 --> 00:34:39,746
But people who live alone,
I've heard they've stopped eating.

494
00:34:41,873 --> 00:34:46,170
And so many have jumped from the stairs
that they've started hanging nets.

495
00:34:46,837 --> 00:34:52,176
I swear if I ever f-forgot Allie,
I'd follow them over the side."

496
00:34:54,803 --> 00:34:57,431
And that was his last entry.

497
00:34:59,391 --> 00:35:01,143
[Juliette] I see what you're doing, and...

498
00:35:02,769 --> 00:35:06,189
Yeah. It's fucking sad,
but I-- I told you,

499
00:35:06,190 --> 00:35:08,774
I'm not killing a thousand people.

500
00:35:08,775 --> 00:35:12,111
Salvador Quinn,
the head of IT at the time,

501
00:35:12,112 --> 00:35:15,948
chose to drug the Silo
to prevent a Safeguard.

502
00:35:15,949 --> 00:35:19,410
He never questioned the need
to erase people's memories,

503
00:35:19,411 --> 00:35:21,205
but he also never denied the cost.

504
00:35:21,788 --> 00:35:27,376
Quinn calculated that the terror and
disorientation from the forgetting drugs

505
00:35:27,377 --> 00:35:30,506
resulted in the death of 2,000 people.

506
00:35:32,925 --> 00:35:36,929
If you do not act, that is the cost.

507
00:35:38,472 --> 00:35:43,060
I know you don't want to be the one
to make this choice.

508
00:35:43,644 --> 00:35:44,770
- I know.
- Do you?

509
00:35:45,812 --> 00:35:49,525
But the burden is on you to do it.

510
00:36:06,124 --> 00:36:07,751
- You all right?
- [Charlotte] Yeah.

511
00:36:09,878 --> 00:36:12,588
[Anna] You seem a little restless
back here.

512
00:36:12,589 --> 00:36:13,590
Uh-huh.

513
00:36:16,218 --> 00:36:18,011
Maybe you don't like flying.

514
00:36:18,762 --> 00:36:19,888
I don't.

515
00:36:20,931 --> 00:36:22,307
Unless it's me doing the flying.

516
00:36:23,225 --> 00:36:26,185
- [chuckles] So a control freak?
- No, not at all.

517
00:36:26,186 --> 00:36:28,020
I just like things done the right way.

518
00:36:28,021 --> 00:36:29,188
[chuckles]

519
00:36:29,189 --> 00:36:32,483
- What about you? You're not sleeping?
- Uh, it's just...

520
00:36:32,484 --> 00:36:35,571
[inhales deeply] It's a big day tomorrow.

521
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:38,364
Yeah. What are we going to see?

522
00:36:38,365 --> 00:36:40,075
You'd be awful at a magic show.

523
00:36:40,742 --> 00:36:42,618
You'd be like, "Stop with the theatrics."

524
00:36:42,619 --> 00:36:44,913
- Yeah, just get to the damn rabbit.
- [chuckles]

525
00:36:57,926 --> 00:36:59,386
Can I get you anything?

526
00:37:00,637 --> 00:37:02,264
- No.
- [chuckles] Okay.

527
00:37:03,974 --> 00:37:05,976
But you could rub my feet.

528
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:19,573
[grunts]

529
00:37:21,450 --> 00:37:22,533
[sighs]

530
00:37:22,534 --> 00:37:26,412
- Sorry about, you know, earlier.
- No. Yeah.

531
00:37:26,413 --> 00:37:28,247
I mean, I thought
you were Charlotte's doctor.

532
00:37:28,248 --> 00:37:30,875
- Now I find out you're a part of this.
- No, no, no. I...

533
00:37:30,876 --> 00:37:35,296
No, I am Charlotte's doctor.
And I'm still gonna need your help

534
00:37:35,297 --> 00:37:37,423
in restoring
her childhood memories especially--

535
00:37:37,424 --> 00:37:40,218
What if she only ever remembers
the last two weeks?

536
00:37:40,219 --> 00:37:41,552
No, it will work.

537
00:37:41,553 --> 00:37:43,346
Yet you said there was no guarantees.

538
00:37:43,347 --> 00:37:46,600
I did, but two things can be true at once.

539
00:37:47,226 --> 00:37:49,645
She's making great progress,
she really is.

540
00:37:50,646 --> 00:37:51,855
[Daniel clicks tongue]

541
00:38:00,155 --> 00:38:02,366
You know, when I was living
in Los Angeles,

542
00:38:02,866 --> 00:38:07,245
all my daughter, Lulu, could talk about
was the Grand Canyon.

543
00:38:07,246 --> 00:38:08,247
[stammers]

544
00:38:08,747 --> 00:38:11,875
Begged us every day
for a month to go see it.

545
00:38:13,210 --> 00:38:14,920
Finally, her mom and I caved in.

546
00:38:15,546 --> 00:38:18,257
One Friday night after work
we took off on a road trip...

547
00:38:20,759 --> 00:38:23,845
I was trying to get to the South Rim,

548
00:38:23,846 --> 00:38:25,388
you know, before the sun rose.

549
00:38:25,389 --> 00:38:28,141
I just kept thinking
how much Lulu would just love that.

550
00:38:28,892 --> 00:38:30,561
Yeah. [chuckles]

551
00:38:33,981 --> 00:38:37,651
I shouldn't have pushed so hard, you know.
I... I was tired.

552
00:38:39,403 --> 00:38:40,988
So was Kevin Bishop.

553
00:38:42,823 --> 00:38:47,870
And I didn't see his truck drift across
the center line until it was too late.

554
00:38:52,291 --> 00:38:56,502
Daniel, all the techniques
that I'm using on Charlotte,

555
00:38:56,503 --> 00:38:58,546
the drugs, the therapy,

556
00:38:58,547 --> 00:39:03,177
I devised all of it 30 years ago
for myself because I wanted to forget.

557
00:39:03,844 --> 00:39:07,805
- And I did, all of it--
- Yeah, but you didn't. You just told me.

558
00:39:07,806 --> 00:39:11,351
Because we discovered in time
that you could choose...

559
00:39:12,769 --> 00:39:15,522
you could choose what to keep,
what to leave behind.

560
00:39:16,815 --> 00:39:18,817
According to the EMT report...

561
00:39:24,323 --> 00:39:26,325
[mournful music playing]

562
00:39:28,911 --> 00:39:31,997
...I held my daughter in my arms
as she died.

563
00:39:36,793 --> 00:39:39,755
I didn't want to remember that.
And I haven't.

564
00:39:40,589 --> 00:39:41,882
It works.

565
00:39:51,308 --> 00:39:52,351
[Helen] Where are we?

566
00:40:01,360 --> 00:40:04,279
[exhales deeply] Um, Atlanta.

567
00:40:09,451 --> 00:40:11,953
Don't forget,
Anthony has to wear a jacket today.

568
00:40:11,954 --> 00:40:13,037
[Anthony] Mom, no!

569
00:40:13,038 --> 00:40:16,249
In your last class portrait,
you were all in jackets.

570
00:40:16,250 --> 00:40:17,959
[Anthony] That was five years ago.

571
00:40:17,960 --> 00:40:19,294
You looked very smart.

572
00:40:19,795 --> 00:40:21,337
The artist did a wonderful job.

573
00:40:21,338 --> 00:40:23,130
- [Sims] Camille.
- What?

574
00:40:23,131 --> 00:40:24,758
Don't make him.

575
00:40:26,009 --> 00:40:27,802
This is your decision now?

576
00:40:27,803 --> 00:40:29,638
I was thinking it was his.

577
00:40:35,853 --> 00:40:37,020
Fine.

578
00:40:38,522 --> 00:40:39,690
Whatever.

579
00:40:50,784 --> 00:40:52,077
[knocks on door]

580
00:40:54,246 --> 00:40:56,498
Your mom must've forgot her keys.

581
00:40:58,917 --> 00:41:00,209
[tense music playing]

582
00:41:00,210 --> 00:41:01,962
What the fuck are you doing here?

583
00:41:03,046 --> 00:41:06,592
- I told Nichols no more contact.
- Yeah, she said there's no camera here.

584
00:41:07,092 --> 00:41:09,218
We need passes for Judicial.

585
00:41:09,219 --> 00:41:10,761
For what?

586
00:41:10,762 --> 00:41:13,848
Look, she didn't say, but she wouldn't
have me climb a hundred levels

587
00:41:13,849 --> 00:41:15,434
if she didn't need them.

588
00:41:19,563 --> 00:41:23,649
[Sims] Anthony, we got a change in plans.

589
00:41:23,650 --> 00:41:25,193
You're gonna take the jacket.

590
00:41:25,194 --> 00:41:27,487
- Dad, you just said--
- I just said take it.

591
00:41:29,114 --> 00:41:32,116
If no one else wears one,
you don't have to.

592
00:41:32,117 --> 00:41:37,289
Your mom is having a hard time at work,
and this'll make her happy.

593
00:41:38,207 --> 00:41:39,208
Okay?

594
00:41:41,293 --> 00:41:42,710
[worker] I have three of these in stock.

595
00:41:42,711 --> 00:41:45,214
Excuse me. Sorry. I need a school jacket.

596
00:41:46,131 --> 00:41:48,132
All right. Uh, what size?

597
00:41:48,133 --> 00:41:49,926
Uh, I don't, uh...

598
00:41:49,927 --> 00:41:51,303
Well, he's about this tall.

599
00:41:54,765 --> 00:41:56,432
We're gonna be late.

600
00:41:56,433 --> 00:41:59,561
Sorry about that.
I can carry the jacket if you want.

601
00:42:01,772 --> 00:42:03,315
We'll get there on time, okay?

602
00:42:04,983 --> 00:42:06,359
- Excuse me?
- Yes?

603
00:42:06,360 --> 00:42:08,319
This is for Anthony Sims.

604
00:42:08,320 --> 00:42:11,155
The judge just realized he gave him
the wrong one this morning.

605
00:42:11,156 --> 00:42:13,616
[stammers] I'm sure
the one Anthony has will be fine.

606
00:42:13,617 --> 00:42:16,369
Uh, that's his old one. It won't fit.

607
00:42:16,370 --> 00:42:17,620
[exhales heavily]

608
00:42:17,621 --> 00:42:18,704
Could I get the other one?

609
00:42:18,705 --> 00:42:20,624
The judge would like to get it
to Recycling.

610
00:42:21,333 --> 00:42:22,459
Just a sec.

611
00:42:27,089 --> 00:42:28,465
- [Shirley] Did you get 'em?
- Here.

612
00:42:28,966 --> 00:42:29,967
[Shirley] Great.

613
00:42:30,509 --> 00:42:31,635
[Juliette] Thanks.

614
00:42:34,930 --> 00:42:36,014
They gonna work?

615
00:42:36,682 --> 00:42:37,807
- Yeah.
- Yeah?

616
00:42:37,808 --> 00:42:38,891
They're great.

617
00:42:38,892 --> 00:42:40,560
- [panting]
- You okay?

618
00:42:40,561 --> 00:42:42,854
You know the last time
I did that many stairs in one day?

619
00:42:42,855 --> 00:42:44,814
Never, I've never done that.

620
00:42:44,815 --> 00:42:46,316
[airplane landing]

621
00:42:55,450 --> 00:42:57,619
- [Daniel] Here we go. Okay.
- [Helen] Okay, this is me.

622
00:42:59,872 --> 00:43:01,372
Helicopters?

623
00:43:01,373 --> 00:43:04,083
Yeah, the next part of our tour
requires we go slow and low.

624
00:43:04,084 --> 00:43:05,459
And where are we going?

625
00:43:05,460 --> 00:43:07,628
A property of mine not too far away.

626
00:43:07,629 --> 00:43:11,174
It's 300,000 acres.
That's 400 square miles.

627
00:43:11,175 --> 00:43:14,635
It used to consist of 42 individual farms.

628
00:43:14,636 --> 00:43:17,556
You convinced 42 Georgia farmers
to give up their land?

629
00:43:18,640 --> 00:43:20,683
I gave them generational wealth

630
00:43:20,684 --> 00:43:23,728
that will take care of
their grandchildren's grandchildren.

631
00:43:23,729 --> 00:43:24,938
[Daniel inhales deeply]

632
00:43:27,900 --> 00:43:31,110
I gotta ask, that rumor going around?

633
00:43:31,111 --> 00:43:32,945
[sighs] I am not a robot.

634
00:43:32,946 --> 00:43:34,740
No, the other one.

635
00:43:35,449 --> 00:43:38,492
- The unicorn.
- Oh, that one's true.

636
00:43:38,493 --> 00:43:40,995
[Anna] Mom, we'll take the one
at the back, okay?

637
00:43:40,996 --> 00:43:44,123
- I thought that was a joke.
- No, no, we have the science.

638
00:43:44,124 --> 00:43:47,084
We created our own
gene-editing technology.

639
00:43:47,085 --> 00:43:49,837
It makes CRISPR look like Fisher-Price.

640
00:43:49,838 --> 00:43:54,634
We did some magic with horse,
narwhal and rhino DNA.

641
00:43:54,635 --> 00:43:57,094
It would've worked too, but I scrapped it.

642
00:43:57,095 --> 00:43:58,471
[scoffs]

643
00:43:58,472 --> 00:44:01,517
So this mysterious whatever-it-is
on Georgia land,

644
00:44:02,017 --> 00:44:04,185
how much of it's your idea,
and how much of it's his?

645
00:44:04,186 --> 00:44:08,564
He's the brains. I'm just a believer,
doing whatever I can to help.

646
00:44:08,565 --> 00:44:14,237
So, hopefully, once you learn
a little bit more, you'll become one too.

647
00:44:14,238 --> 00:44:16,155
[helicopter whirring]

648
00:44:16,156 --> 00:44:17,241
Let's go.

649
00:44:21,954 --> 00:44:25,874
You had a momentary burst
of not wanting to be a mad scientist?

650
00:44:26,458 --> 00:44:29,460
I talked with the tenth richest man
in the world.

651
00:44:29,461 --> 00:44:33,631
And he offered me a billion dollars
if after I created my unicorn,

652
00:44:33,632 --> 00:44:35,217
he could hunt and kill it.

653
00:44:36,468 --> 00:44:37,970
Fucking billionaires.

654
00:44:38,929 --> 00:44:40,764
They ruin everything!

655
00:44:50,899 --> 00:44:51,900
[door opens]

656
00:44:52,901 --> 00:44:54,026
Hey.

657
00:44:54,027 --> 00:44:55,611
Oh, you took your jacket?

658
00:44:55,612 --> 00:44:56,738
Dad made me.

659
00:44:57,614 --> 00:44:58,740
How'd it go?

660
00:44:59,408 --> 00:45:02,034
I was the only one
who even brought a jacket.

661
00:45:02,035 --> 00:45:03,119
How do you think?

662
00:45:03,120 --> 00:45:04,203
- Excuse...
- Hey--

663
00:45:04,204 --> 00:45:05,330
- Hey.
- Anthony!

664
00:45:07,249 --> 00:45:08,625
I'll talk to him.

665
00:45:11,295 --> 00:45:12,670
[inhales sharply]

666
00:45:12,671 --> 00:45:15,506
After dinner,
I need your help with something.

667
00:45:15,507 --> 00:45:18,217
I have to go to Judicial Seclusion.

668
00:45:18,218 --> 00:45:21,138
The nurse who jabbed Emerson is there,
and I need to talk to her.

669
00:45:21,930 --> 00:45:23,640
I'd like you there as a witness.

670
00:45:26,643 --> 00:45:28,228
- All right.
- Okay.

671
00:45:33,984 --> 00:45:36,110
And thanks for getting him
to take the jacket.

672
00:45:36,111 --> 00:45:37,738
- [Sims] Of course.
- Mm-hmm.

673
00:45:40,407 --> 00:45:42,700
[Teddy, on radio]
<i>Donny calling for the dispatch.</i>

674
00:45:42,701 --> 00:45:44,203
Go ahead, Donny.

675
00:45:44,828 --> 00:45:47,038
We're heading out to make the delivery.

676
00:45:47,039 --> 00:45:48,497
[Martha] <i>Copy.</i>

677
00:45:48,498 --> 00:45:50,501
[suspenseful music playing]

678
00:46:23,951 --> 00:46:25,244
[shushes]

679
00:46:34,211 --> 00:46:36,713
[tense music playing]

680
00:46:48,475 --> 00:46:49,768
[gasps]

681
00:46:54,314 --> 00:46:55,773
- [radio beeps]
- Yeah.

682
00:46:55,774 --> 00:46:59,610
It's Donny again. I got a bit
of an issue here with the delivery.

683
00:46:59,611 --> 00:47:01,445
[Martha] What's the problem, Donny?

684
00:47:01,446 --> 00:47:03,824
[Teddy] <i>I just saw two big dogs
go into the apartment.</i>

685
00:47:04,491 --> 00:47:06,617
<i>I mean, really big.</i>

686
00:47:06,618 --> 00:47:08,953
- What does that mean?
- [Sandy] Dogs.

687
00:47:08,954 --> 00:47:10,746
Isn't that what people call
Judicial goons?

688
00:47:10,747 --> 00:47:12,790
- [Juliette] What?
- [Sandy sighs] Does he mean Sims?

689
00:47:12,791 --> 00:47:15,502
- His wife?
- What? No, no, no, no, no. No.

690
00:47:17,546 --> 00:47:18,922
[Bernard exhales deeply]

691
00:47:20,966 --> 00:47:22,550
We-We're not...

692
00:47:22,551 --> 00:47:24,636
Are you sure you'll get another chance?

693
00:47:27,431 --> 00:47:28,473
[mutters]

694
00:47:29,600 --> 00:47:30,893
[sighing]

695
00:47:35,939 --> 00:47:37,232
Just... [sighs]

696
00:47:38,317 --> 00:47:39,359
Just go.

697
00:47:41,612 --> 00:47:42,737
Copy that, Donny.

698
00:47:42,738 --> 00:47:44,239
<i>Proceed to delivery.</i>

699
00:47:52,080 --> 00:47:54,290
[suspenseful music playing]

700
00:47:54,291 --> 00:47:55,292
What's this?

701
00:47:56,043 --> 00:47:59,880
Boss has us servicing all the pumps,
level by level.

702
00:48:03,383 --> 00:48:06,427
- It takes six of you?
- Any other levels? Nope.

703
00:48:06,428 --> 00:48:09,264
But for Judicial we were told
to get it done in one night.

704
00:48:17,773 --> 00:48:20,359
[tense music playing]

705
00:48:42,130 --> 00:48:43,131
[Juliette] Hey.

706
00:48:48,887 --> 00:48:50,264
[Bernard clears throat]

707
00:49:09,074 --> 00:49:10,200
[Kennedy, on radio] <i>Hey.</i>

708
00:49:10,868 --> 00:49:12,411
Yeah. Donny, what is it?

709
00:49:12,911 --> 00:49:14,705
[Kennedy] <i>Who the fuck is Donny?</i>

710
00:49:18,083 --> 00:49:19,168
Who is this?

711
00:49:20,878 --> 00:49:22,170
[Kennedy] <i>This is Deputy Calso.</i>

712
00:49:22,171 --> 00:49:26,300
- <i>Um, here on, um... level 17.</i>
- [whispers] Patrick.

713
00:49:27,050 --> 00:49:32,346
Uh, Calso, this is, um, Deputy... uh, Crane.
Please continue.

714
00:49:32,347 --> 00:49:33,764
<i>Dep... Deputy Crane, yeah, okay.</i>

715
00:49:33,765 --> 00:49:38,103
<i>So, uh, we had a little bit of a situation
here but, um, the kids are all right.</i>

716
00:49:41,481 --> 00:49:44,817
Donny? Donny, do you hear me?
This is dispatch.

717
00:49:44,818 --> 00:49:48,906
<i>Your delivery is no longer necessary.
Please return to base.</i>

718
00:49:49,489 --> 00:49:50,573
[Teddy] Shit!

719
00:49:50,574 --> 00:49:53,451
- [Kennedy stammers] <i>Who... Who's Donny?</i>
- Oh. Calso, will you get off the line?

720
00:49:53,452 --> 00:49:55,453
Please shut up. Donny, did you get that?

721
00:49:55,454 --> 00:49:56,871
- [pants]
- Do you read me?

722
00:49:56,872 --> 00:49:58,664
Your delivery is no longer necessary--

723
00:49:58,665 --> 00:50:00,958
Stop! Stop!

724
00:50:00,959 --> 00:50:02,669
[Martha] Please return to base.

725
00:50:04,254 --> 00:50:07,716
- [crew member] Stop! Stop!
- [Teddy] Stop! Stop! Stop!

726
00:50:09,343 --> 00:50:10,344
- Stop!
- Stop!

727
00:50:12,721 --> 00:50:14,764
[pants] This is Donny.

728
00:50:14,765 --> 00:50:18,477
- Yes. Go ahead, Donny.
- <i>It's off. Returning to base.</i>

729
00:50:21,396 --> 00:50:22,648
[sighs]

730
00:50:24,441 --> 00:50:27,652
[panting] Yes.

731
00:50:27,653 --> 00:50:28,736
[sighs]

732
00:50:28,737 --> 00:50:31,573
Martha, do you have anything to drink?

733
00:50:32,574 --> 00:50:34,409
[Martha] I'll break out the good stuff.

734
00:50:35,244 --> 00:50:37,079
[sighs, breathes shakily]

735
00:50:37,579 --> 00:50:38,580
[mutters]

736
00:50:40,499 --> 00:50:43,793
Calso. C-Calso, this is, um, Crane.

737
00:50:43,794 --> 00:50:45,170
Can, uh... Can you hear me?

738
00:50:53,679 --> 00:50:54,762
[chains clanking]

739
00:50:54,763 --> 00:50:57,390
Could you please tell me what's going on?

740
00:50:57,391 --> 00:50:59,559
[breathes heavily] Please.
What's gonna happen to me?

741
00:50:59,560 --> 00:51:02,228
Amy, you need to shut the fuck up. Okay?

742
00:51:02,229 --> 00:51:04,021
Camille, what are we doing here?

743
00:51:04,022 --> 00:51:05,274
[Camille] What are we doing here?

744
00:51:08,652 --> 00:51:12,906
I needed somewhere
where we couldn't be seen or heard

745
00:51:13,657 --> 00:51:14,992
because we need to talk.

746
00:51:16,994 --> 00:51:18,412
- Okay.
- [Camille] Okay.

747
00:51:19,329 --> 00:51:23,541
I asked you to stop, Robert,

748
00:51:23,542 --> 00:51:24,751
and you didn't.

749
00:51:25,252 --> 00:51:27,128
I really don't know
what you're talking about.

750
00:51:27,129 --> 00:51:29,715
You used our son!

751
00:51:30,883 --> 00:51:33,844
The jacket Anthony came home with?

752
00:51:34,469 --> 00:51:35,596
Not his.

753
00:51:40,976 --> 00:51:42,352
Yeah.

754
00:51:43,020 --> 00:51:44,021
[scoffs]

755
00:51:49,776 --> 00:51:53,988
If you could stop putting
the forgetting drugs in the water--

756
00:51:53,989 --> 00:51:55,239
What are you doing?

757
00:51:55,240 --> 00:51:57,825
- That's all I'm asking you, is to stop.
- What are you doing, Rob?

758
00:51:57,826 --> 00:52:01,245
- Tell me. Tell me what you're doing.
- You need to stop. Just stop.

759
00:52:01,246 --> 00:52:02,956
- This drug could--
- Tell me what you're doing!

760
00:52:04,666 --> 00:52:07,753
Can we discuss this somewhere other
than in front of her?

761
00:52:08,295 --> 00:52:10,464
Oh, Amy? Fuck Amy.

762
00:52:11,256 --> 00:52:13,633
- No, no! [cries] Please!
- [Sims] No, no. No!

763
00:52:13,634 --> 00:52:16,219
- No. Please, don't-- Please!
- Tell me the truth. Tell me the truth.

764
00:52:16,220 --> 00:52:17,721
- Please.
- [crying]

765
00:52:19,848 --> 00:52:21,350
Like you haven't done as much.

766
00:52:24,770 --> 00:52:26,313
I have done worse.

767
00:52:28,190 --> 00:52:29,399
I have.

768
00:52:30,692 --> 00:52:33,570
Whether you're right or... or I'm right...

769
00:52:36,657 --> 00:52:39,368
Whether our son remembers me or not...

770
00:52:42,579 --> 00:52:49,336
he deserves to have at least one parent
who hasn't killed someone.

771
00:52:54,716 --> 00:52:57,010
[helicopter whirring]

772
00:53:01,723 --> 00:53:02,766
[sighs]

773
00:53:03,642 --> 00:53:04,768
[sighs]

774
00:53:09,398 --> 00:53:10,524
What the hell is that?

775
00:53:12,150 --> 00:53:13,193
[Thurman] Daniel knows.

776
00:53:18,824 --> 00:53:21,368
[dramatic music playing]

777
00:53:25,497 --> 00:53:26,707
Um...

778
00:53:28,917 --> 00:53:29,918
It's a drill.

779
00:53:31,587 --> 00:53:34,798
It's a very big drill.

